.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS VA_LIST
.IP
.EX
\f[B]typedef\f[R] va_list;
\f[B]typedef\f[R] VA_LIST;
.EE
.PP
Provides an uppercase alias for the standard \f[CR]va_list\f[R] type.
.PP
\f[CR]VA_LIST\f[R] can be used to declare a variable\-argument list
object without referring directly to the implementation\-provided
\f[CR]va_list\f[R] type.
.PP
The lowercase \f[CR]va_list\f[R] remains available as provided by the
underlying variable\-argument implementation.
.SS Example
.IP
.EX
VA_LIST args;

VA_BEGIN(args, count);
.EE
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS VA_ARG
.IP
.EX
#define VA_ARG
#define va_arg
.EE
.PP
Retrieves the next argument from a variable\-argument list.
.PP
\f[CR]VA_ARG\f[R] is the uppercase form of \f[CR]va_arg\f[R] and follows
the behavior of the active variable\-argument implementation.
.PP
The first argument specifies the variable\-argument list, while the
second specifies the expected type of the next argument.
.SS Example
.IP
.EX
int value;

value = VA_ARG(args, int);
.EE
.PP
The lowercase form can be used equivalently:
.IP
.EX
value = va_arg(args, int);
.EE
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS VA_COPY
.IP
.EX
#define VA_COPY
#define va_copy
.EE
.PP
Copies the current state of a variable\-argument list into another
variable\-argument list.
.PP
This is useful when the same argument sequence needs to be traversed
more than once.
.PP
\f[CR]VA_COPY\f[R] is the uppercase form of \f[CR]va_copy\f[R] and uses
the implementation supplied by the compiler or the fallback provided by
\f[CR]VA_ARGS.H\f[R].
.SS Example
.IP
.EX
VA_LIST copy;

VA_COPY(copy, args);
.EE
.PP
The copied list should be terminated independently:
.IP
.EX
VA_END(copy);
.EE
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS VA_BEGIN
.IP
.EX
#define VA_BEGIN
#define va_begin
.EE
.PP
Initializes a variable\-argument list before its arguments are accessed.
.PP
The exact behavior is provided by the active variable\-argument
implementation.
.SS Example
.IP
.EX
VA_LIST args;

VA_BEGIN(args, count);

value = VA_ARG(args, int);

VA_END(args);
.EE
.PP
The lowercase form is equivalent:
.IP
.EX
va_begin(args, count);
.EE
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS VA_END
.IP
.EX
#define VA_END
#define va_end
.EE
.PP
Terminates a variable\-argument list after it has been used.
.PP
Every variable\-argument list initialized with \f[CR]VA_BEGIN\f[R] or
otherwise initialized according to the active implementation should be
passed to \f[CR]VA_END\f[R] when it is no longer needed.
.SS Example
.IP
.EX
VA_LIST args;

VA_BEGIN(args, count);

\f[I]/* Use args. */\f[R]

VA_END(args);
.EE
.PP
The lowercase form can be used equivalently:
.IP
.EX
va_end(args);
.EE
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS VA_ARGS
.IP
.EX
#define VA_ARGS
#define va_args
.EE
.PP
Represents the variable\-argument portion of a function declaration.
.PP
\f[CR]VA_ARGS\f[R] provides a portable abstraction for declaring
functions that accept a variable number of arguments.
It is intended to serve the role of the standard \f[CR]...\f[R] syntax
or the \f[CR]va_alist\f[R] convention used by older variable\-argument
implementations.
.PP
This allows a function declaration to use the library\(aqs
variable\-argument syntax without directly depending on a particular
compiler\(aqs declaration convention.
.SS Example
.IP
.EX
int print_values(int count, VA_ARGS);
.EE
.PP
On implementations where standard C variable arguments are available,
\f[CR]VA_ARGS\f[R] can correspond to the appropriate
\f[CR]...\f[R]\-style declaration.
.PP
On implementations requiring an alternative variable\-argument
convention, \f[CR]VA_ARGS\f[R] can instead be mapped to the
corresponding implementation\-specific representation.
.RS
.PP
\f[B]Note:\f[R] \f[CR]VA_ARGS\f[R] is a declaration keyword provided by
\f[CR]VA_ARGS.H\f[R]; it should not be confused with the C
preprocessor\(aqs \f[CR]__VA_ARGS__\f[R] macro.
.RE
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS VA_DECLARE
.IP
.EX
#define VA_DECLARE
#define va_declare
.EE
.PP
Declares or prepares the variable\-argument portion of a function
according to the active \f[CR]VA_ARGS.H\f[R] implementation.
.PP
\f[CR]VA_DECLARE\f[R] provides a portable abstraction for the
variable\-argument declaration mechanism required by implementations
that cannot use the standard \f[CR]...\f[R] syntax directly.
.PP
It is intended as the library\(aqs equivalent of the declaration
operation used by the underlying variable\-argument system, including
implementations based on the older \f[CR]va_dcl\f[R] convention.
.SS Example
.IP
.EX
int print_values(count)
    int count;
    VA_DECLARE
{
    VA_LIST args;

    VA_BEGIN(args, count);

    \f[I]/* Process variable arguments. */\f[R]

    VA_END(args);
}
.EE
.PP
The lowercase form is equivalent:
.IP
.EX
va_declare
.EE
.PP
The actual expansion of \f[CR]VA_DECLARE\f[R] depends on the compiler
and variable\-argument implementation selected by \f[CR]VA_ARGS.H\f[R].
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS Usage
A typical variable\-argument function can be written using the portable
interface provided by the header:
.IP
.EX
#include \f[B]<VA_ARGS.H>\f[R]

int sum(int count, VA_ARGS)
{
    VA_LIST args;
    int     total;
    int     i;

    total = 0;
    VA_BEGIN(args, count);

    i = 0;
    \f[B]while\f[R] (i < count)
    {
        total += VA_ARG(args, int);
        i++;
    }

    VA_END(args);
    \f[B]return\f[R] (total);
}
.EE
.PP
The same interface is available in lowercase form:
.IP
.EX
#include \f[B]<VA_ARGS.H>\f[R]

int sum(int count, va_args)
{
    va_list args;
    int     total;
    int     i;

    total = 0;
    va_begin(args, count);

    i = 0;
    \f[B]while\f[R] (i < count)
    {
        total += va_arg(args, int);
        i++;
    }

    va_end(args);
    \f[B]return\f[R] (total);
}
.EE
.PP
Using the uppercase interface is useful when the source code is intended
to make library\-provided portability abstractions visually distinct
from the underlying compiler implementation.
